-- Passage wg11-narrative-writing-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ce1c39f0-3b8d-5408-9e55-8e95fc81e268', 'wg11-narrative-writing-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-narrative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Tag Number 4417$q$, $q$(1) The meadow behind the county nature center was so loud with crickets that I could hear it before I saw it. (2) I had signed up to help Dr. Farah Siddiqui, a biologist who tags migrating monarch butterflies each September. (3) Each tag is a tiny round sticker with a code, placed on the underside of a wing so that anyone who finds the butterfly later can report where it traveled. (4) My job was to hold the net and pass Dr. Siddiqui the tags in order.

(5) My first three swings caught nothing but goldenrod. (6) My mentor laughed. (7) "Slow down," Dr. Siddiqui said, "and let them come to the flowers." (8) I stood still for what felt like ten minutes, sweat running into my eyes, until a monarch landed on a thistle near my elbow.

(9) Butterflies taste with their feet. (10) Afterwards, I swept the net in one smooth arc. (11) The butterfly fluttered against the mesh, orange and black and impossibly light. (12) Dr. Siddiqui showed me how to hold it gently by the closed wings and press the sticker, number 4417, onto the wing.

(13) I opened my fingers. (14) For a second, the monarch just sat on my palm. (15) Then it lifted, circled once above the goldenrod, and drifted south over the treetops.

(16) In conclusion, monarchs are interesting insects.$q$, 'draft')
