        '9-12', $q$Saving Old Films$q$, $q$(1) Many films made in the first half of the twentieth century survive only as fragile reels of nitrate or acetate stock, and archivists race to preserve them before they decay beyond repair. (2) Acetate film, for instance, can develop a condition nicknamed "vinegar syndrome," in which the base releases acetic acid and gradually shrinks, curls, and becomes brittle. (3) Once this process begins, it accelerates, because the acid released by the film speeds up further breakdown.

(4) Restoration typically begins with a careful physical inspection. (5) Technicians unwind each reel by hand, repair torn perforations, and clean away dust and mold with soft brushes and specialized solvents. (6) ____ the film is stable enough to run through a machine is it scanned frame by frame at very high resolution. (7) The resulting digital files then become the raw material for the most painstaking stage of the work. (8) Movie theaters of that era were often decorated with elaborate painted ceilings and velvet curtains.

(9) During digital restoration, specialists remove scratches, correct flickering brightness, and rebuild damaged frames using information from neighboring ones. (10) This work requires restraint as well as skill. (11) A restorer who smooths away every trace of grain may produce an image that looks cleaner than the original ever did, which misrepresents how audiences first experienced the film. (12) ____, the goal is not perfection but faithfulness: the restored film should look the way its makers intended on the day it premiered.$q$, 'draft')
