-- Passage wg11-paragraph-transitions-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d0a1c759-f6e2-5053-8ace-2a2c9ee7fd74', 'wg11-paragraph-transitions-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Rowing as One$q$, $q$(1) To spectators on the riverbank, a racing eight seems to glide effortlessly, but the smoothness of its motion depends on a coordination problem that rowers spend years trying to solve. (2) Each of the eight athletes pulls an oar, and the boat moves fastest when all eight blades enter and leave the water at precisely the same instant. (3) If one rower catches the water even a fraction of a second early, that blade acts briefly as a brake, and the hull loses speed before surging forward again. (4) ____, these tiny interruptions can cost a crew several seconds over a two-kilometer race, enough to separate first place from fourth.

(5) Coaches address the problem in several ways. (6) The most familiar is repetition: crews row thousands of strokes together until the shared rhythm feels automatic. (7) ____, many programs now attach small sensors to each oar that record the force and timing of every stroke. (8) By comparing these records, a coach may discover that one athlete consistently finishes the stroke late, a flaw nearly invisible from a following boat. (9) Some rowing clubs also host social gatherings for former members every spring.

(10) Yet technology alone cannot produce a unified crew. (11) Rowers must also learn to set aside their individual styles, matching a teammate's rhythm even when their own instinct suggests a different pace. (12) The stroke seat, the rower nearest the stern, sets this rhythm, and the others follow it closely. (13) A crew that masters this shared timing gains more speed than any single powerful rower could provide.$q$, 'draft')
