   $q$Why is "Despite these obstacles" a better way to begin sentence 12 than "As a result of these obstacles"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The two phrases mean the same thing, so the choice depends only on the writer's style.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-logical-relationship"},{"text":"The interviews were completed in spite of the problems in sentences 10 and 11, not because of them, so a concession fits.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The obstacles caused the volunteers to work harder, so the phrase should show cause and effect.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-cause-effect"},{"text":"Sentence 12 introduces a new set of obstacles, so it needs a phrase that adds information.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-reference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The interviews were completed in spite of the problems in sentences 10 and 11, not because of them, so a concession fits.$q$, $q$Sentences 10 and 11 describe problems that worked against the project, and sentence 12 reports success anyway, which calls for a concession like "Despite." The choice claiming the obstacles made volunteers work harder is not supported anywhere in the passage.$q$, 'draft', 'ac862c71-796b-5d6a-ae3d-73f8ecea601d')
