-- Passage wg11-paragraph-transitions-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('387933c9-eda9-5abe-b3cd-1da3644a93d9', 'wg11-paragraph-transitions-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$How Devices Fix Their Own Mistakes$q$, $q$(1) Every time a file is saved, copied, or sent across a network, there is a small chance that some of its bits, the ones and zeros that encode it, will be flipped by electrical noise or physical wear. (2) A single flipped bit might turn a letter in a document into a different character or leave a visible blotch in a photograph. (3) ____, engineers have developed error-correcting codes that allow a device to detect and repair such mistakes automatically.

(4) The basic idea is to store slightly more information than the data strictly requires. (5) The simplest version, called a parity bit, adds one extra digit to each group of bits so that the total number of ones is always even. (6) If a single bit flips, the count becomes odd, and the device knows that something has gone wrong. (7) A parity bit can reveal that an error exists, ____ it cannot reveal which bit is wrong, so it cannot fix the problem on its own. (8) Early computers filled entire rooms and required teams of operators to run them.

(9) More sophisticated codes overcome this limitation by adding several carefully arranged extra bits. (10) Each extra bit checks a different, overlapping group of data bits, so the pattern of failed checks points to the exact location of the error. (11) The device can then flip that bit back, restoring the original data without the user ever noticing. (12) These codes are a quiet but essential part of modern technology, protecting everything from the files on a laptop to the signals sent home by distant space probes.$q$, 'draft')
