  ('4f518bf8-140e-5b0c-860c-bdf6854870c2', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-precise-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$In a paragraph about a lake ferry, the first two sentences both begin with "The ferry." Which revision of the third sentence, "The ferry stops running when ice forms on the lake in January," varies the opening while keeping the meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The ferry stops running in January when ice forms on the lake.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"starts-with-subject"},{"text":"Ice forms on the lake in January, the ferry stops running.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice"},{"text":"When ice forms on the lake in January, the ferry stops running.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The ferry, in January, when ice forms on the lake, stops running.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"starts-with-subject"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$When ice forms on the lake in January, the ferry stops running.$q$, $q$Moving the "when" clause to the front changes the opening and keeps the cause clear. "Ice forms on the lake in January, the ferry stops running" also starts differently, but it joins two sentences with only a comma.$q$, 'draft', null)
