   $q$Which revision of sentence 11 would best conclude the draft?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Solberg's team found leafy lichens on 64 percent of the trees in a park two miles inland from the harbor highway.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"evidence-without-analysis"},{"text":"Lichens are clearly the most accurate way to measure air pollution, so cities can stop buying air sensors.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"},{"text":"Solberg's survey shows that leafy lichens vanish near pollution and return when the air improves, making them a free record of a city's air.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Many people walk past lichens every day without noticing them, which is a shame because they are beautiful.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"off-topic-opinion"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Solberg's survey shows that leafy lichens vanish near pollution and return when the air improves, making them a free record of a city's air.$q$, $q$A strong conclusion restates the claim and ties together both kinds of evidence, disappearing near pollution and returning after improvement. The choice about replacing air sensors goes far beyond anything Solberg's survey shows.$q$, 'draft', '27392148-b5b7-5528-9db6-194b6a6c773f')
