-- Passage wg11-writing-with-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('61903b11-9d54-544c-a03c-0167e95daf4a', 'wg11-writing-with-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: The School at Lock Nine$q$, $q$(1) Historian Kwame Adjei's essay "The School at Lock Nine" tells the story of a night school that operated beside the Merrow Canal from 1846 to 1871. (2) Adjei argues that the school lasted twenty-five years because its founders built it around the lives of canal workers instead of expecting the workers to adjust. (3) The founders held classes only in winter, when ice closed the canal and the boat crews had no work. (4) Adjei also points out that the first lessons taught arithmetic through cargo weights and toll charges, problems the students faced every week on the water. (5) By tying lessons to work the students already knew, the founders gave tired crews a practical reason to attend.

(6) Attendance records support Adjei's reading. (7) According to Adjei, enrollment grew from nine students in the first winter to more than eighty by 1855. (8) Many of these students were teenagers who had left village schools early to work on the boats. (9) Canal boats in that period were usually pulled by mules walking along a towpath. (10) "I learned to read the toll book myself, and no clerk could cheat me after that." (11) ____$q$, 'draft')
