        '9-12', $q$Draft: A Teacher's Thermometer$q$, $q$(1) In the article "A Teacher's Thermometer," science writer Mira Castellanos describes how researchers are using the weather diaries of Lucinda Ferrante, a schoolteacher who recorded conditions at Lake Tamsin every day from 1871 to 1912. (2) Castellanos shows that careful amateur records can become valuable scientific evidence when researchers test them against other sources. (3) Ferrante wrote down the temperature each morning at seven o'clock and noted the date the lake froze each winter. (4) Climate researcher Oluwaseun Bello compared her readings with records from a government weather station forty miles away and found that the two sets of numbers rose and fell together almost exactly. (5) This match matters because it shows that Ferrante's thermometer and habits were reliable, not just that she was hardworking. (6) Bello's team then used the diaries to extend the lake's ice record back by forty years. (7) Castellanos reports that the lake now freezes, on average, about three weeks later than it did in Ferrante's time.

(8) Castellanos also describes the challenges. (9) In some years Ferrante was away visiting family, and her students took the readings for her. (10) Bello found that those weeks contained more errors, so his team removed them before finishing the analysis. (11) Ferrante also taught music and directed the town's children's choir.$q$, 'draft')
