  ('c8e5d29c-ec03-53ae-b98a-94182cf34271', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-argument-writing'), null, 'hard',
   $q$A skeptical reader might say that sentence (3) proves little, since students who seek internships may already be unusually focused. Which revision of sentence (3) best answers this concern?$q$, $q$[{"text":"At Carver Ridge High, 68 percent of the enthusiastic interns reported that the program helped them choose a path.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"emotional-wording"},{"text":"At Carver Ridge High, a large share of interns said the program was helpful in planning their futures.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"vaguer-evidence"},{"text":"At Carver Ridge High, 68 percent of interns, compared with 41 percent of seniors with similar grades who did not intern, said they had chosen a major or training program.","is_correct":true},{"text":"At Carver Ridge High, nearly every intern said the experience changed their life in remarkable ways.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstated-evidence"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$At Carver Ridge High, 68 percent of interns, compared with 41 percent of seniors with similar grades who did not intern, said they had chosen a major or training program.$q$, $q$Comparing interns with similar seniors who did not intern helps show the internship, not the students' prior focus, made the difference. The vaguer choice removes the number and does nothing to address the reader's doubt.$q$, 'draft', '24233b5d-ff09-5478-9b65-8360200f0e4d')
