   $q$A critic notes that sentence (5) shows only that the elective is popular, not that it teaches anything valuable. Which revision of sentence (5) best connects this evidence to the claim in sentence (2)?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Last spring, more students signed up for the darkroom elective than ever before in the school's history.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"still-popularity-only"},{"text":"Last spring, 46 students signed up for the darkroom elective, though some later switched to other courses.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"undercuts-argument"},{"text":"Last spring, 46 students took the darkroom elective, and their teacher found that they explained exposure on the final exam more accurately than students in the digital course.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Last spring, a remarkable 46 students eagerly signed up for the darkroom elective, a truly impressive number.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"emotional-wording"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Last spring, 46 students took the darkroom elective, and their teacher found that they explained exposure on the final exam more accurately than students in the digital course.$q$, $q$Exam results about exposure show learning, which is what sentence (2) claims the darkroom provides. The record enrollment choice still measures only popularity, so it does not answer the critic.$q$, 'draft', '242328e9-3dd5-568d-a0d2-763fe7f08981')
