  ('54e1cbeb-4529-5e5a-8320-df8e074e834f', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-argument-writing'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The writer wants to add a sentence after sentence (3) that addresses a weakness in that evidence without abandoning the claim. Which choice does this best?$q$, $q$[{"text":"These results prove that the air at every bus stop in Tilbury is unsafe to breathe.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes"},{"text":"Because the study was so small, its findings should probably be ignored by the city.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"abandons-claim"},{"text":"The students who ran the project were very hardworking and deserve recognition.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-praise"},{"text":"Four stops over a single month is a small sample, which is why a citywide network is needed to see whether the pattern holds across seasons.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Four stops over a single month is a small sample, which is why a citywide network is needed to see whether the pattern holds across seasons.$q$, $q$The correct choice admits the sample is small and turns that limit into a reason for the proposed network. Saying the findings should be ignored names the weakness but gives up the evidence instead of answering it.$q$, 'draft', 'fd561ad4-6a2e-585c-b1ba-633844e72912')
