   $q$Read the sentence: "The planetarium staff trained a group of first year volunteers to run the evening star shows." Which revision correctly punctuates the modifier before "volunteers"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The planetarium staff trained a group of first, year volunteers to run the evening star shows.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-inside-compound-modifier"},{"text":"The planetarium staff trained a group of first-year volunteers to run the evening star shows.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The planetarium staff trained a group of first year-volunteers to run the evening star shows.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"hyphen-joins-noun"},{"text":"The planetarium staff trained a group of first-year-volunteers to run the evening star shows.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"hyphen-joins-noun"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The planetarium staff trained a group of first-year volunteers to run the evening star shows.$q$, $q$"First-year" is a compound adjective placed before the noun "volunteers," so its two words are joined by a hyphen. The noun stays separate, which is why "first-year-volunteers" is wrong.$q$, 'draft', null)
