   $q$Which sentence about the radio telescope uses a semicolon correctly?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The radio telescope collected signals all night; and filled three drives with data by morning.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-before-predicate"},{"text":"The radio telescope collected signals all night; by morning, the data filled three drives.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The radio telescope collected; signals all night, and the data filled three drives by morning.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-splits-clause"},{"text":"Although the radio telescope collected signals all night; the data filled three drives by morning.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-after-dependent-clause"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The radio telescope collected signals all night; by morning, the data filled three drives.$q$, $q$A semicolon joins two complete independent clauses, and both "The radio telescope collected signals all night" and "by morning, the data filled three drives" can stand alone. A semicolon after the dependent "Although" clause separates a fragment from its main clause.$q$, 'draft', null)
