        '9-12', $q$Where Donated Clothes Go$q$, $q$(1) Each year, residents of the city of Brennick donate roughly 9,000 tons of clothing to collection bins and charity shops. (2) Many donors assume that every item will be worn again by someone nearby. (3) In reality, donated clothing follows several different paths, and only a fraction is resold locally.

(4) At the Brennick Reuse Center, workers sort donations by hand into dozens of categories. (5) According to the center's 2024 annual report, about 20 percent of donated garments were clean and sturdy enough to sell in its own shops. (6) Another 45 percent were sold in large bales to dealers who resell used clothing in other regions. (7) The rest went somewhere else.

(8) Recycling textiles is harder than recycling paper or aluminum. (9) Most garments are made of blends, such as cotton mixed with polyester, and separating these fibers is difficult and costly. (10) Mechanical recyclers shred fabric into short fibers, which are usually too weak to spin into new clothing and are used instead for insulation, padding or industrial rags. (11) A newer method, chemical recycling, dissolves certain fibers so they can be rebuilt into material as strong as new, but only a few pilot plants currently use it.

(12) So that is what happens to clothes.$q$, 'draft')
