-- Passage wg12-narrative-writing-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('45ab10b5-fea1-5c1f-9c7a-06ea5df02e70', 'wg12-narrative-writing-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-narrative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Riffle Count$q$, $q$(1) Our environmental science class waded into Miller Creek on a gray October morning, each pair carrying a kick net and a white plastic tray. (2) Ms. Nakamura had explained the plan the day before: we would disturb the gravel upstream, let the current wash whatever lived there into our nets, and count the insects we caught. (3) Certain larvae, like mayflies and stoneflies, cannot survive in polluted water, so finding them would suggest the creek was healthy.

(4) My partner, Jun, held the net while I shuffled my boots through the rocks. (5) The water was so cold it made my ankles ache. (6) We lifted the net and dumped its contents into the tray. (7) Mud. (8) A leaf. (9) A single snail. (10) Many people enjoy fishing in creeks during the fall. (11) "Try the riffle," Ms. Nakamura called from the bank, pointing to a stretch where the water broke white over shallow stones.

(12) We moved and tried again. (13) This time, when the silt settled, the tray seemed to come alive. (14) Tiny flattened bodies with three tails crawled along the bottom, and a stonefly larva as long as my thumbnail clung to a twig. (15) Jun counted out loud while I recorded each one in the notebook, my handwriting shaky from the cold. (16) We found forty-one mayflies in that sample alone.

(17) The class results showed the creek was in good condition.$q$, 'draft')
